      "body": "Confirmed reproducible. This is not primarily a Homarr bug.\n\nHomarr\u2019s Uptime Kuma integration only uses the public status page API (`/api/status-page/{slug}` and `/api/status-page/heartbeat/{slug}`). Monitor status is derived from the latest heartbeat per monitor \u2014 not from Uptime Kuma\u2019s dashboard pause state (`active` flag).\n\nWhen you pause a monitor in the Uptime Kuma dashboard, polling stops and no new heartbeat is written. The last heartbeat (usually up) remains, so Homarr still counts it as up. Homarr\u2019s \u201cPaused\u201d counter only reflects heartbeat status 2 (pending/retrying), 3 (maintenance), or monitors with no heartbeat \u2014 not dashboard pause.\n\nThis matches Uptime Kuma\u2019s own status page behavior. Paused monitors are not shown as paused on public status pages either; that\u2019s a known upstream limitation: [louislam/uptime-kuma#2287](https://github.com/louislam/uptime-kuma/issues/2287).\n\nHomarr also has no dedicated background cron job for this widget (1-minute on-demand cache), but even with perfect polling the paused count would not change until pause state is exposed via the API or Homarr uses an authenticated endpoint that includes monitor.active.\n\nSeems to be a valid bug/UX mismatch \u2014 the \u201cPaused\u201d label implies dashboard pause, but the implementation cannot detect it with the current data source.",
